with nearly six decades of tv series and  films under its belt star track  is a vaunted franchise that always  inspires fan discussion on the best  entries in the series  here's why star trek six the  undiscovered country is the best film  embodiment of the star trek world  creator gene roddenberry imagined star  trek as a wagon train to the stars with  earth settlers boldly exploring the  final frontier of space  but he also conceived of it as a cold  war allegory one in which the united  federation of planets represented nato  the klingon empire stood in for the  soviet union and the romulans were the  red communist chinese  a number of episodes of the original  series allegorized cold war conflicts  including  errand of mercy friday's child elon of  troyeus  the omega glory and balance of terror  producer hart bennett's holy trilogy the  wrath of khan  the search for spock and the voyage home  explored the relationships and trials of  the enterprise crew and the sacrifices  they made for one another  but when paramount brought back wrath of  khan writer director nicholas meyer to  make one  final film with the original series cast  he and leonard nimoy took star trek back  to its cold war roots  imagining the fall of the berlin wall in  space and its aftermath specifically  they wanted to dramatize the hard work  of achieving peace in a world used to  hate  paranoia and xenophobia the film's plot  about a coup  that attempts to derail a galactic peace  conference mirrored the real events  unfolding in the summer of 1991 so  accurately including russian hardliners  trying to restore the old soviet union  that paramount actually tried to rush  the film into theaters before it was  ready  maybe because he started out as a  novelist nicholas meyer has a real gift  with dialogue  sure he borrows some of the dialogue in  his star trek movies from classic works  of western civilization but he's good at  using those works to underscore his  themes  star trek 2 for example opens with spock  giving kirk an  antique copy of dickens a tale of two  cities a novel about sacrifice  later in the film kirk quotes the novel  reflecting on spock's sacrifice for the  greater good  in the undiscovered country meyer has  the klingon character chang lay claim to  shakespeare and even weaves in  references to brave new world sherlock  holmes and peter pan  but meyer also writes excellent original  dialogue the undiscovered country is  about people confronting the prejudice  of their own thinking  and the way in which kirk and spock  acknowledge their own shortcomings and  mistakes is very poignant  they're a great one for logic i'm a  great one for  rushing in where angels fear to tread a  bit later in the film spock asks  is it possible that we too you and i  have become so old and inflexible that  we have  outlived our usefulness it's a very  self-reflective dialogue that allows for  meaningful change  making undiscovered country a film with  exciting character development  a script with plenty of shakespearean  dialogue demands shakespearean actors  and undiscovered country delivers  royal shakespeare company veteran david  warner plays chancellor gorkhan  a klingon ambassador trying to forge  peace with the federation  the great christopher plummer meanwhile  stars as the villainous klingon general  chang who spearheads a conspiracy to  sabotage the peace process  by this time plummer was as famous as a  shakespearean actor on the stage as he  was for his roles on screen  he had made his thespian bones in  canadian theater working with none other  than william shatner  the future captain kirk was plumber's  understudy in wait for it  henry v so when playing chang plummer is  right at home  intending quotes from hamlet henry v  julius caesar  and the merchant of venice while he  taunts kirk and company  to be or not to be that is the question  which preoccupies our people captain  quick  while plummer gives a famously  flamboyant performance as chang he is  fully committed to the role giving it  the dramatic heft it deserves  in fact plummer had been a star trek fan  when the show debuted in the 1960s  despite the fact that industrial light  and magic handled visual effects for  both the star wars and star trek movies  their star wars effects became better  known partly because of star trek's  association with low budget aesthetic in  the original tv show  but the movies featured some impressive  spectacles the space battle inside the  pink clouds of the mutara nebula in  wrath of khan  the enterprise self-destructing and  burning up in the search for spock  and the klingon bird of prey hovering  over a whaling ship in the voyage home  undiscovered country is the most  sustained visual achievement of the  original movie series  it out does those previous films in  several ways as with the mammoth opening  explosion of the klingon mining moon and  the giant purple shockwave that engulfs  sulu's ship the excelsior  there's a terrific action sequence set  on a gravity-disabled klingon flagship  when two masked assassins  gunned down the klingons on board  sending their fuchsia blood floating in  neon globules through the zero-g  environment  star trek vi is also bursting with alien  masks  and makeup putting on a huge prosthetic  show and giving the galaxy  a sense of being diversely populated  finally meyer and cinematographer hero  narita elect for low lighting throughout  which gives the film  a burnished menacing glow every star  trek movie either introduces new  characters or delves deeper into  characters from the original tv series  and the quality of the films often  mirrors how well this is done  take the arch villain khan who made his  classic cinema debut in star trek ii  and contrasts that with spock's  forgettable half-brother cybok  introduced in the wretched star trek 5.  star trek vi  balances out the main cast with a wealth  of memorable new faces beyond chang and  gorkon including gorkhan's fierce  daughter  ozzie bohr played by rosanna desoto as  well as the grandfather of star trek the  next generation's wharf  both played by michael dorn other  sharply drawn characters include a  shape-shifting alien named martia  played by the model iman and kirkwood  smith as the federation president  even christian slater shows up for a  cameo but the best new character is a  pre-sex and the city kim cattrall as  lieutenant valeris  spock's vulcan protege belarus has more  scenes than most of the trek regulars  and the movie  is better for the freshness and vitality  of her character she  and chang along with many other new  characters helped make the star trek  universe feel densely populated  novelizations and other material in the  trek universe had long established the  promotion  to captain of sulu played by george  takei which fans had been clamoring to  see on film for quite some time  his ship the uss excelsior had actually  been introduced in star trek iii the  search for spock  as one of a new generation of starships  that would replace the enterprise  when kirk goes rogue in that movie  starfleet sends the excelsior to  apprehend him but it fails because  scotty has sabotaged the transwarp drive  but fans found the ship cool and they  finally got their wish of seeing sulu in  charge of it when it showed up under his  command  a few films later in undiscovered  country narratively adding another  starship  widens the scope of the film and it  gives one of the primary original  characters something to do besides hang  around on the enterprise bridge  sulu and the excelsior feature  prominently in the dramatic opening of  the film  as well as the exciting climax as they  race at warp 10 to help the enterprise  save the galactic peace conference a  junior officer warns sulu about the  speed to which his commander barks  fly her apart then you can practically  feel takei's relish at getting a line  this exciting after yes siring captain  kirk all those years  sulu's expanded role fits well into the  fast exciting storytelling on display in  the undiscovered country  a sherlock holmes fan writer director  meyer who also wrote the seven percent  solution  infuses a mystery story into the larger  space opera plot as the enterprise crew  races against the clock to figure out  who has framed them for an  act of international terrorism that  jeopardizes a peace conference meyer and  his co-writers leonard nimoy and denny  martin flynn also add a juicy courtroom  trial  an elaborate banquet scene and a prison  escape for good measure  along with all the space battles phaser  fights and philosophical discussions  fans had come to expect  in star trek 2 spock saves the  enterprise and her crew at the expense  of his life  in star trek 3 the enterprise crew  returns the favor but sacrifices the  ship  in star trek 4 the stakes include the  survival of earth and all its people  and by the time we get to the  undiscovered country the fate of the  entire galaxy hangs in the balance  can humans and alien species work  together to preserve the fragile goal of  peace  or is the galaxy doomed to perpetual war  these are the highest stakes of the  series so far  making for great tension the personal  stakes are high in this movie too  whereas the characters had to save each  other individually in previous outings  in undiscovered country they've got to  save themselves as a group  will they be able to see the error of  their ways before it's too late they  certainly try spock admits that it was  quote arrogant presumption on his part  that got them into the story's mess in  the first place as well as his belief in  the inherent superiority of vulcans  kirk meanwhile confesses that he was so  used to fighting klingons it never  occurred to him to believe they might  actually want peace  saying gorkan had to die before i  understood how prejudiced i was  by the end of the film he sees them in a  new light and has even earned their  trust  people can be very frightened of change  inside the starships in the undiscovered  country feel more like tightly packed  ships at sea or even submarines than  through the brightly lit flying living  rooms of the next generation this fits  with gene roddenberry's original vision  of starfleet as a navy with the officers  using nautical directions like port and  starboard and of course the uss  enterprise was meant to be the  descendant  of the many real ship's designated  enterprise including the famous world  war ii aircraft carrier  meyer embraced roddenberry's vision of  star trek as fictional british naval  hero horatio hornblower voyaging in  space  in star trek 6 the director expands that  concept even more fully than he did in  star trek 2  putting the crewmen together in cramped  bunk rooms referring to the kitchen  as the galley and using torpedoes during  warfare  meyer epitomizes the naval theme  beautifully with kirk's great final line  when asked for a course heading he  paraphrases peter pan saying  second star to the right and straight on  till morning of course peter pan was an  adventure set on ships but the line also  suggests the eternal pull of the horizon  which had always informed the hopeful  vision of star trek  it's now expected for popular franchises  to wink at their fans referencing  elements outside of a given film's  particular story but when star trek vi  came out in 1991 the practice was pretty  rare and the film trailblazed in that  area too  at one point when scotty suggests that  their situation is so  dire that they're practically dead spock  quips  i've been dead before meanwhile as the  film tackles the theme of aging it  directly engages the cultural jokes  about the enterprise crew  saving the galaxy in walkers and  wheelchairs but nods to the franchise's  history are never at the expense of the  film's central story  the only responsibility the filmmakers  feel is to make a good final movie  especially after the lackluster star  trek five  the sixth installment grandly sends off  the original cast and crew  but it's also a send-off to the fans who  made it all possible their passion saved  star trek from cancellation and kept it  alive through some pretty weak movies  with star trek 6 the original series  ends on a high note its greatest gift to  fans is saving the best for last  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  star trek are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

